<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Maria Olbrich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Maria Olbrich was an Austrian architect and a founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-secession">Vienna Secession</a>, whose exhibition building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> he designed in 1897-1898. Invited by Grand Duke Ernest Louis to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt-artists-colony">Darmstadt Artists' Colony</a> in 1899, he designed most of its buildings on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathildenhohe">Mathildenhöhe</a>, including the Wedding Tower of 1908. He was a leading figure of Jugendstil architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-maria-olbrich.jpg" alt="Joseph Maria Olbrich" width="320" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Maria_Olbrich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Maria Olbrich was born on 22 December 1867 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Troppau</a>, in Austrian Silesia, now Opava in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, the third child of Edmund and Aloisia Olbrich. His father was a prosperous confectioner and wax manufacturer who also owned a brick works, where Olbrich's interest in the construction industry has its early origin. He studied architecture at the University of Applied Arts Vienna and the Academy of Fine Arts Vienna, winning several prizes, among them the Prix de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, for which he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1893 Olbrich began working for Otto Wagner on the buildings of the Wiener Stadtbahn. In 1897 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-klimt">Gustav Klimt</a>, Olbrich, Josef Hoffmann and Koloman Moser founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-secession">Vienna Secession</a>. Its aesthetic sought playful juxtapositions of blank surfaces and geometric regularity against expansive, largely floral, ornament. Olbrich's Secession Building combined a church-like entrance hall with a warehouse-like exhibition space and became the movement's landmark.</p><p>In 1899 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-louis-grand-duke-of-hesse">Ernest Louis, Grand Duke of Hesse</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt-artists-colony">Darmstadt Artists' Colony</a>, for which Olbrich designed many houses and several exhibition buildings. He gained Hessian citizenship in 1900 and was appointed to a professorship by the Grand Duke. He married Claire Morawe in 1903. He also designed pottery, furniture, book bindings and musical instruments, and his courtyard and interiors at the St. Louis World's Fair won the highest prize there. His other works include a house for Hermann Bahr in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> store for Leonhard Tietz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> and the Villa for Josef Feinhals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, built in 1908 and destroyed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shortly after the birth of his daughter Marianne on 19 July 1908, Olbrich died of leukaemia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> on 8 August, aged 40. His exhibition buildings for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and Darmstadt Secessions had a strong influence on the development of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a> style.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph Maria Olbrich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Louis made Darmstadt a centre of modern design by founding the Darmstadt Artists' Colony on the Mathildenhöhe in 1899; its exhibition of 1901, with buildings by <b>Joseph Maria Olbrich</b> and Peter Behrens, was a landmark of Jugendstil and early modern architecture, crowned in 1908 by the Wedding Tower.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.94,17.9&amp;year=1893" aria-label="Opava on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4960 426 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joseph Maria Olbrich"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5410 696h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5410 696h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.94,17.9&amp;year=1893">What was here in 1893? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
